WN.lb(27,{"mt_t0RfQnvh3G":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Kit jumps up and down. How can you play with her?","choices":["Jump too","Sit down and look away","Take her shoes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Watch Kit, then do the same thing she does.","explain":"Jumping too copies Kit, and copying is a way to play together."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You put brick number {n} on the tower. Who goes next?","choices":["Kit","Me again","Nobody"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take turns: me, then Kit.","explain":"After my go, it is Kit's go. Taking turns lets both of us build one tower.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Kit has the teddy. You want a go. What do you do?","choices":["Ask, \"Can I have a go?\"","Grab it","Hit Kit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use kind words to ask, not your hands.","explain":"Asking for a go is kind, and Kit can pass the teddy when she is ready. Grabbing makes her sad."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia and Kit build one big tower. Are they playing together?","choices":["Yes, together","No, next to each other","No, they are not playing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there one tower or two?","explain":"They share one tower and add bricks in turn, so they are playing together."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia and Kit each do their own puzzle, side by side. What kind of playing is that?","choices":["Playing next to each other","Playing together","Not playing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does each one have her own puzzle?","explain":"Each has her own puzzle, so they are playing next to each other. That is OK too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Kit asks for the red brick. You have a car and a red brick. What do you give her?","choices":["The red brick","The car","Nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen: which toy did Kit ask for?","explain":"Kit asked for the red brick, so passing her that is the right toy and helps the game."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You roll the ball to Kit {n} times. She rolls it back each time. Are you playing together?","choices":["Yes","No","Only Kit is playing"],"answer":0,"hint":"The ball goes to Kit and back again.","explain":"Rolling a ball back and forth is a shared game, so we are playing together.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Kit is rolling a ball. You just watch. Does Kit know you want to play?","choices":["No, I need to ask","Yes, she always knows","Yes, if I stare"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kit cannot see inside your head. How can she find out?","explain":"Kit cannot guess what I want. Asking \"Can I play?\" tells her."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Kit claps her hands. How can you play with her?","choices":["Clap too","Walk away","Shout \"Stop!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Watch what Kit does. Can you do the same?","explain":"Copying a friend is one of the first ways children play together, so clapping too joins Kit's game."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Kit has put {n} bricks on the tower. She just had a go. Whose turn is it now?","choices":["My turn","Kit's turn again","Nobody's turn"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kit just went. Who goes after Kit?","explain":"Taking turns means my go, then Kit's go. Kit just had her go, so now it is my turn.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Kit says, \"Can I have the ball?\" What do you do?","choices":["Give Kit the ball","Hide the ball","Give Kit the car"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen to what Kit asked for.","explain":"Kit asked nicely for the ball. Passing her the toy she asked for is a kind way to play together."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia and Kit play side by side. Each has {n} crayons and her own picture. Is that OK?","choices":["Yes, it is OK","No, they must share","No, they are cross"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do they look upset, or just busy?","explain":"Playing next to a friend is a normal stage for small children and is how playing together starts.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Kit is playing chase. You want to join. What do you say?","choices":["\"Can I play?\"","Nothing, just push","\"Go away!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Use kind words to join in.","explain":"Asking \"Can I play?\" lets Kit know you want to join, and she can say yes."}],"explain":{"kid":["You can play next to a friend. You have your toys. Kit has her toys. That is OK.","You can play with a friend too. Kit claps. You clap too! Now you play together.","Build one tower. You put a brick on. Then Kit puts one on. That is taking turns.","Kit asks for the ball. Give it to her. Want to join a game? Ask, \"Can I play?\" Do not grab."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 2 to 3, is about moving from playing alongside other children to playing with them. Most two-year-olds play next to a friend with their own toys and watch what the friend does. This parallel play is a normal stage, not a problem to fix. Over the next year, short shared moments grow from it: copying, swapping a toy, building one thing together, or a simple chase.","In the lab, your child copies what Kit does, takes turns to build one tower, passes Kit the toy she asks for, and chooses how to join her ball game (watch, grab, or ask \"Can I play?\"). Then they roll the ball back and forth and sort pictures into playing next to each other or playing together.","Common mistakes: expecting a two-year-old to share on demand, which leads to tears, and treating side-by-side play as a sign that something is wrong. It is easier to start with turn-taking games that have a clear \"my go, your go\", and to give them words such as \"Can I have a go?\" than to force a toy into another child's hands. Praise the moments when they pass, copy or wait.","Play together at home with one shared thing: one tower, one ball, one jigsaw. Say whose turn it is out loud, and let your child see you ask, wait and say thank you."]},"familyTip":"Roll a ball back and forth with your child and say \"my go, your go\" each time. Then invite a teddy or a sibling to join, and let your child show them how to take turns.","keyIdea":"Young children first play next to each other, each with their own toys, and that is normal. Step by step they start to play together: copying a friend, taking turns, passing a toy, asking \"Can I play?\" and sharing one game such as building a tower or rolling a ball.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Next to or together? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Next to","Together"],"items":[["each has own crayons",0],["each has own puzzle",0],["each has own book",0],["build one tower",1],["roll a ball back and forth",1],["play chase",1],["pass a toy",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagplay","caption":"Flagship lab: copy Kit, take turns on one tower, pass the toy she asks for, ask to join her ball game, then sort next to or together.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Ag9NSWJu-X":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Leo says, \"It doesn't matter, I don't mind,\" but he does mind. Which style is this?","choices":["Passive","Assertive","Aggressive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is Leo saying what he really needs?","explain":"Leo hides what he needs, so it is passive. Being quiet and nice is not the same as being assertive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Someone takes Zoe's seat. She shouts, \"Move, or you'll be sorry!\" Which style is this?","choices":["Aggressive","Assertive","Passive"],"answer":0,"hint":"A threat: is it calm, or does it attack?","explain":"A threat attacks the other person, so it is aggressive, even if Zoe was right to want her seat back."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam has asked {n} times to have a turn. Which reply is assertive?","choices":["\"I've asked a few times. Please can I have a turn now?\"","\"Fine, I don't want a turn anyway.\"","\"You're so selfish. Give it to me!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the reply that is calm, clear and kind.","explain":"The first reply says what Sam needs, calmly. The second gives in (passive) and the third blames and grabs (aggressive).","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia says, \"I feel like you are mean.\" Why is this not a good I-statement?","choices":["It is really a blame about the person","It does not start with I","It is too short"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mean is a word about the person. Is it a feeling?","explain":"Mean is not a feeling, it is a label for the other person. A good I-statement names a feeling, like sad, and the action: \"I feel sad when you laugh at me.\""},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Two friends talk it through and agree to take turns on the bike, {n} minutes each. What is the agreement called?","choices":["The resolution","The conflict","The boundary"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is this the problem, or the fix?","explain":"The fix that ends a conflict is the resolution. The conflict was the disagreement about the bike.","params":{"n":[5,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Raj says, \"Please ask before you borrow my things.\" What is Raj setting?","choices":["A boundary","A conflict","A compromise"],"answer":0,"hint":"Raj is drawing a line for how others treat his things.","explain":"A boundary is a line you set for how others treat you or your things. Saying it calmly is assertive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Being assertive means...","choices":["Saying what you need, calmly and with respect","Getting your own way every time","Keeping quiet to stay out of trouble"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the middle of the line from quiet to loud.","explain":"Assertive means you say what you need, calmly, and you respect the other person too. It does not mean you always win."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which word means two sides talking it through to find a deal?","choices":["Negotiate","Mediate","Aggressive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a helper in the middle, or just the two sides?","explain":"To negotiate is for the two sides to talk it through. To mediate is for a third person to help them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Eli wants the blue team. Kim wants the red team. Which is a compromise?","choices":["Eli plays blue today and red next time","Kim gives in, and Eli gets his way","They both stop playing for good"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do both of them get some of what they want?","explain":"Taking turns means each gives up a little and both get some. One person giving in is not a compromise."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Jo takes Kai's pencil. Kai says, \"That's my pencil. Please give it back.\" Which style is this?","choices":["Assertive","Passive","Aggressive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is Kai calm? Does he say what he needs?","explain":"Kai is calm and clear, and he says what he needs. That is assertive."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ruby waits {n} minutes for the swing. Then Max pushes in. Ruby says nothing and walks away. Which style is this?","choices":["Passive","Assertive","Aggressive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Ruby say what she needed?","explain":"Ruby went quiet and gave in, so her need was never said. That is passive.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which one is an I-statement?","choices":["I feel upset when you talk over me.","You always talk over me.","You are so rude."],"answer":0,"hint":"An I-statement starts with I and names a feeling.","explain":"\"I feel upset when you talk over me\" says the feeling and the action, with no blame. The others start with you and blame."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ava and Ben both want all {n} minutes of break: Ava for tag, Ben for football. Which plan is a compromise?","choices":["Half the break for each game","Ben gives in and plays tag","Ava shouts until Ben agrees"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a compromise, does one person give in, or do both give a little?","explain":"Sharing the time means both give up a little and both get some. If one gives in, that is not a compromise.","params":{"n":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Two friends argue. A teacher stands between them and helps each one listen. What is the teacher doing?","choices":["Mediating","Negotiating","Being passive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the teacher one of the two sides, or a helper in the middle?","explain":"A helper in the middle who helps both sides listen is mediating. Negotiating is when the two sides talk it out."}],"explain":{"kid":["When someone upsets you, there are three ways to answer. Passive means you go quiet or give in. Aggressive means you shout, blame or threaten. Assertive is in the middle: you say what you need, calmly, and you are kind about it.","Assertive is not the same as loud. Shouting \"Give it back, thief!\" makes the other person feel attacked, so they fight back. Saying \"That's my pencil. Please give it back\" is calm and clear, and it works better. Being quiet and nice is not assertive either, if you never say what you need.","An I-statement is a good way to be assertive. Say how you feel, what they did, and what you need: \"I feel sad when my book gets lost. Could we look for it together?\" Start with I, not you, and talk about what happened, not about what the person is like.","A conflict is a disagreement. You negotiate when you talk it through to find a deal. A helper in the middle can mediate. A compromise is when both of you give up a little, so you both get some of what you want. The fix you agree on is the resolution. A boundary is a line you set, like \"Please ask before you take my things.\""],"adult":["This topic is for ages 7 to 11. It gives children the words for healthy talk when things go wrong: passive, assertive and aggressive styles, I-statements, conflict, negotiate, mediate, compromise, resolution and boundary. With the words, a child can name what happened and choose a better way next time.","The three styles are best taught as one line, from quiet to loud, with assertive in the middle. Passive means the need is never said, so the problem stays. Aggressive means the need is said as an attack, so the other person defends. Assertive means the need is said clearly and with respect for both people. An I-statement (\"I feel ... when ... Could we ...?\") is the usual tool taught in schools.","Common mistakes: thinking assertive means loud or getting your own way; thinking being quiet and nice is the same as being assertive; \"I feel like you are rude\", which is a blame with I in front; using always and never, which start an argument; and thinking a compromise means one person gives in. Children also mix up negotiate (the two sides talk) and mediate (a third person helps).","In the lab your child slides Kai's reply from passive to aggressive and watches Jo react, sorts six replies into the three styles, builds two I-statements from pieces, shares a 20-minute break between tag and football until both get a fair part, and matches five conflict words to scenes."]},"familyTip":"When a squabble comes up, pause and ask: \"Was that passive, assertive or aggressive?\" Then help your child try it again as an I-statement: \"I feel ... when ... Could we ...?\" Praise the calm, clear version, even if it does not get them their way.","keyIdea":"There are three ways to speak up. Passive means you go quiet or give in, so no one hears what you need. Aggressive means you shout, blame or threaten, so the other person feels attacked. Assertive means you say what you need, calmly and with respect. An I-statement helps: \"I feel upset when you talk over me. Could we take turns?\" A conflict is a disagreement. You negotiate by talking it through, a helper can mediate, and a compromise is when both give up a little. The fix you agree on is the resolution.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"I-statement or you-blame? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["I-statement","You-blame"],"items":[["I feel sad when my game gets knocked over.",0],["I feel left out when I'm not picked.",0],["I feel cross when you take my pens.",0],["You always ruin everything.",1],["You are so annoying.",1],["You never listen to me.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagassert","caption":"Flagship lab: slide a reply from passive to aggressive, sort replies, build I-statements, find a compromise, match conflict words.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QXnZ5TPCib":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Mo has no toy to play with. You have three cars. What is kind?","choices":["Offer Mo a car","Keep all three cars","Hide the cars"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who has nothing? What could you give them?","explain":"Offer Mo one car. Mo has something to play with, and you still have two."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You want a turn on the swing. What can you say?","choices":["Can I have a turn next?","Get off, it's mine!","Nothing, just push"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use kind words to ask. Pushing and shouting hurt.","explain":"Ask: can I have a turn next? Then wait. When your friend is done, it is your go."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"You have {n} blocks. You give Kit 1 block to play with. How many blocks do you have now?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take away the one you gave to Kit.","explain":"{n} blocks take away 1 is {=n-1} blocks. And Kit has 1 block too, so you can both build.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Kit has 3 biscuits and you have 1. Is that fair?","choices":["No, not the same","Yes, it is fair","Yes, Kit is bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fair means you both have the same number.","explain":"3 and 1 are not the same, so it is not fair. Deal them one each: 2 for Kit and 2 for you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Pip is having a turn with your ball. You want it back now. What is best?","choices":["Wait for Pip's go to end","Grab it back","Shout at Pip"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pip is only having a turn. Will the ball come back?","explain":"Wait. When Pip's go is done, the ball comes back to you. Grabbing stops Pip's turn."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} children take turns on the slide. Each child has one go. How many goes until everyone has had a go?","answer":"n","hint":"One go for each child. Count the children.","explain":"One go each for {n} children is {n} goes. Then everyone has had a turn.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In a game, Kit had a go and then you had a go. Who goes next?","choices":["Kit","Me","The grown-up"],"answer":0,"hint":"My turn, your turn: the go keeps swapping.","explain":"Kit, then you, so next it is Kit again. The turns swap after every go."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You have the paint pot and your friend has none. What can you say?","choices":["Do you want a turn?","Go away","It's all mine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how your friend feels with nothing to paint with.","explain":"Say: do you want a turn? Then you can take turns with the paint, or both use it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Sharing and taking turns: you just rolled the dice in a game with Kit. Whose turn is it now?","choices":["Kit's turn","My turn again","Nobody's turn"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a game the turn swaps after every go. Who has not just gone?","explain":"You just had your go, so now it is Kit's turn. Then it swaps back to you."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {=2*k} grapes. You share them: one for you, one for Kit. How many grapes does each of you get?","answer":"k","hint":"Deal them out one at a time: one for you, one for Kit, until none are left.","explain":"{=2*k} grapes dealt one each makes {k} for you and {k} for Kit. Same for both, so it is fair.","params":{"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You let Pip have a turn with your teddy. What happens when Pip's go is done?","choices":["The teddy comes back to me","Pip keeps it for ever","The teddy is lost"],"answer":0,"hint":"Having a turn is like borrowing. What happens to things we borrow?","explain":"A turn ends, and then the teddy comes back to you. Sharing is giving a turn, not giving it away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a game, you have {n} goes and Kit has {n} goes. How many goes is that altogether?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count your goes, then count on Kit's goes.","explain":"{n} goes for you and {n} goes for Kit makes {=2*n} goes altogether. You both got the same number.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["In a game, we take turns. I have a go. Then you have a go. Then it is my turn again. Say: my turn, your turn!","When you share your truck, your friend has a turn with it. It does not go away for good. When their go is done, it comes back to you.","Look around. Does a friend have no toy? You can give them one. Say: do you want a turn?","Sharing snacks? Give one to you, then one to your friend. Keep going. Now you both have the same. That is fair."],"adult":["This sits in personal, social and emotional development, under building relationships. The EYFS early learning goal asks children to work and play cooperatively and take turns with others. Development Matters for 3 to 4 year olds names playing with one or more other children and finding solutions to conflicts. The Head Start framework lists cooperative play and turn-taking under relationships with other children. At 3 to 4, expect turn-taking with a grown-up's help, offering a toy now and then, and using the words my turn and your turn.","Three ideas sit underneath. Turn-taking is reciprocity: the go swaps after each move, so each child can predict when their next go comes. Turn-based sharing means lending a toy for a while, which is easier for this age than giving it away, because the toy comes back. Fair sharing is equal shares, made by dealing out one for you and one for me.","Common mistakes: making a child hand a toy over the moment another child asks, which teaches that sharing means losing; expecting a 3-year-old to share well with no help; very long turns, so the waiting child gives up; and praising only the child who gives. Be careful with blame, too. Grabbing at this age is normal. Name the step instead: ask for a turn, then wait for the toy to come back.","In the lab your child taps whose turn it is in a race game, lends Kit a fire truck and watches it come back, offers a toy to the friend who has none, deals out strawberries one each until the plates match, and sorts kind and unkind ways to play."]},"familyTip":"Play a short game with a die or a ball and say each swap out loud: 'My turn… your turn.' When a sibling wants a toy, try 'You can have it when Sam has finished', then make sure it really does come back.","keyIdea":"Taking turns means one person has a go, then the next, then back again: my turn, your turn. Sharing a toy means giving someone a turn with it, and the toy comes back when their go is done. If a friend has nothing to play with, you can offer a toy. When we share out food, one for you and one for me makes it fair, so everyone gets the same.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sharing or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Sharing and turns","Not sharing"],"items":[["Rolling the ball back and forth",0],["Grabbing the bike",1],["Saying: your turn!",0],["Keeping every block",1],["Giving the truck back",0],["Taking two goes in a row",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagshare","caption":"Flagship lab: take turns in a race game, lend a toy and see it come back, offer a toy, share snacks fairly and sort kind play.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YwE34zDyNz":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is a kind way to ask to join in?","choices":["\"Can I play too?\"","Push in","Grab the ball"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kind words, said in a calm voice, help others say yes.","explain":"\"Can I play too?\" uses kind words. Pushing and grabbing spoil the game."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"They say \"Not now.\" What does that mean?","choices":["Not yet, maybe later","They will never play with you","They do not like you"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is \"not now\" the same as \"never\"?","explain":"\"Not now\" means not yet. The game may be full, or they may be in the middle of something."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"They say \"Not now.\" What is a good thing to do?","choices":["Find another game","Knock their game over","Shout at them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick something calm that does not spoil their game.","explain":"Finding another game is calm and kind. You can ask again later."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Children are playing doctors. Which idea fits the game?","choices":["\"I can be the patient!\"","\"Let's play tag!\"","\"I am the boss!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is a part in a doctors game?","explain":"A doctor needs a patient, so that idea fits their game. Tag is a new game, and bossing spoils it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why do you watch before you ask?","choices":["To see what they are playing","To grab a toy fast","To find the boss"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, the apples and coins told Bo something.","explain":"Watching shows you the game, so you can give an idea that fits."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Bo stands near a game but says nothing at all. What can Bo do?","choices":["Ask \"Can I play?\"","Keep quiet all day","Walk off and cry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the children know Bo wants to play?","explain":"If Bo says nothing, the children may not know he wants to play. Kind words tell them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} children play pirates. 2 more children ask \"Can we play?\" and join. How many children play now?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 2 more.","explain":"{n} and 2 more is {=n+2}. The game got bigger because they asked with kind words.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What comes after you ask \"Can I play?\"","choices":["Give an idea for a part","Watch the game","Go home"],"answer":0,"hint":"The steps are watch, ask, give an idea, play.","explain":"After you ask, give an idea, like \"I can be the shopper!\" Then you play together."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"You ask {n} friends to play. 1 says \"Not now.\" The rest say yes. How many say yes?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take the 1 who said \"not now\" away from {n}.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}. One \"not now\" is OK, because others may say yes.","params":{"n":[3,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You want to join a game. What do you do first?","choices":["Watch what they are playing","Grab a toy","Shout \"Me! Me!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Bo looked for clues before he asked. Why did that help?","explain":"Watch first. Then you know the game, and you can ask and give an idea that fits."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{n} children are playing shops. You ask \"Can I play?\" and they say yes. How many children are playing now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Start with the {n} children. You join, so count on 1 more.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. Kind words helped you join the game.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"They are playing trains. Which idea fits their game?","choices":["\"I can be the driver!\"","\"Let's play football!\"","\"Trains are boring.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick a part you can play in their train game.","explain":"A driver is part of the train game, so it shows you want to play with them, not change the game."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You hear \"Not now.\" You take {n} big, slow breaths, then 1 more. How many breaths is that?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count the {n} breaths, then add the 1 extra breath.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. Slow breaths help the sad or cross feeling get smaller.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You see kids playing. You want to play too! First, stop and watch. What are they playing? Who is in the game?","Then use kind words. Say \"Can I play?\" in a calm voice. Do not grab or push in. Grabbing spoils the game.","Give an idea that fits their game. If they play shops, say \"I can be the shopper!\" Now they can see how you fit in.","Some days they say \"Not now.\" That is OK. It does not mean never. Take a big breath. Find a new game, or ask again later."],"adult":["Joining a group that is already playing is one of the hardest social skills for young children. In England it sits in the EYFS prime area Personal, Social and Emotional Development. The early learning goal Building Relationships asks children to work and play cooperatively and take turns with others, and to form friendships with peers. Development Matters suggests helping three- and four-year-olds find solutions to conflicts, for example accepting that not everyone can be Spider-Man in the game and suggesting other ideas. In the US, the Head Start ELOF goals for preschoolers include positive interactions with other children and cooperative play.","Research on group entry (Putallaz and Gottman; Corsaro's work on access rituals) finds the same pattern. Children who join well watch or hover first, then make a comment or offer that fits what the group is already doing. Children who are turned away more often call attention to themselves, change the game or barge in. Key words for this age: watch, ask, idea, join in, take turns, and the phrase \"not now\".","Common mistakes: grabbing a toy or pushing in, shouting \"Me! Me!\", trying to change the game (\"Let's play football instead\"), or hovering silently and never asking. Be careful with the other big slip too: hearing \"not now\" as \"they hate me\" or \"never\", and then giving up or spoiling the game. First refusals are very common, even for well-liked children, and a calm second try or a different group often works.","In the lab your child watches a game of shops and taps the clues that show the game. They sort kind and unkind ways to ask, and drag an idea that fits into three different games. They help Bo the bear after \"not now\" by picking calm next steps, and put watch, ask, give an idea and play in order. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"At the park, watch a group together for a moment first and whisper, \"What are they playing? What part could you be?\" Practise \"Can I play?\" with a soft toy at home, and sometimes have the toy say \"Not now\" so your child can try a calm next step.","keyIdea":"To join a game, watch first to see what the children are playing. Then ask with kind words, like \"Can I play?\", and give an idea for a part in their game. If they say \"Not now\", stay calm. Find something else to do, or ask again later.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"They said \"Not now.\" Good next step or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👍 Good next step","👎 Not a good idea"],"items":[["Find another game",0],["Knock their tower over",1],["Ask again a bit later",0],["Shout \"You are mean!\"",1],["Ask a different friend",0],["Snatch their toy",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagentry","caption":"Flagship lab: watch a game and find the clues, sort ways to ask, give an idea that fits three games, help Bo after \"not now\", and order the join-in steps.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_NS5t-Jzlh8":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does cooperate mean?","choices":["Work together on one job","Do the job all on your own","Tell everyone what to do"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of Fox and Bear lifting the big log.","explain":"To cooperate is to work together on one job, like two friends lifting a log that one friend cannot lift."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is a compromise?","choices":["Each friend gets some of what they want","One friend gets their way","Nobody gets anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the sign painted half red and half blue.","explain":"A compromise is a fair plan: each friend gives a little and gets a part of what they wanted."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Owl is telling Bear an idea. Which shows Bear is listening?","choices":["Bear looks at Owl and waits","Bear plays with a toy","Bear talks over Owl"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listening signs: look, wait, then say back what you heard.","explain":"Looking at Owl and waiting shows Bear is listening. Being quiet with a toy is not the same as listening."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You do not like your friend's idea. Which is a kind way to disagree?","choices":["\"I see it a different way.\"","\"Your idea is silly!\"","\"No! Mine is best!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"You can say what you think and still be kind.","explain":"\"I see it a different way\" tells your friend what you think, calmly. Calling an idea silly hurts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A new child stands on her own at play time. How can you include her?","choices":["Ask her to join your game","Let her watch from far away","Say the game is full"],"answer":0,"hint":"Include means asking someone in, so they are not left out.","explain":"Asking her to join includes her. Watching from far away still leaves her out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"After Owl talks, Bear says, \"You want to build a bridge?\" Why does Bear say that?","choices":["To show he heard Owl's idea","To change the idea","To end the talk"],"answer":0,"hint":"Saying back what you heard is a listening sign.","explain":"Saying it back shows Owl that Bear really heard the idea. It is part of active listening."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} friends are in a team. 2 friends are on their own, so the team asks them to join. How many are in the team now?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 2 more.","explain":"{n} and 2 more is {=n+2}. The team included the 2 friends, so nobody is left out.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Fox wants to paint. Owl wants to build. Fox shouts until Owl gives up. Is that a compromise?","choices":["No, only Fox got his way","Yes, because they stopped arguing","Yes, because Owl agreed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did each friend get some of what they wanted?","explain":"No. Owl gave in, so only Fox got his way. A compromise gives each friend a part, like paint first, then build."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Fox and Owl make a compromise. They play Fox's game for {n} minutes, then Owl's game for {n} minutes. How many minutes do they play in all?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Add Fox's {n} minutes and Owl's {n} minutes.","explain":"{n} + {n} = {=2*n} minutes. Each friend got some of what they wanted, so it is a compromise.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Fox and Owl paint a sign together. Fox paints one half and Owl paints the other half. Which word is that?","choices":["Cooperate","Include","Disagree"],"answer":0,"hint":"They are working on one job together. Which word means that?","explain":"They work together on one job, so they cooperate. Include would mean asking a new friend to join."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{n} friends build a den. 1 friend is on her own, and they ask her to join. How many friends build the den now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Start with the {n} friends. Asking her to join adds 1 more.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. Asking her to join means they include her, so nobody is left out.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Mia wants to play tag. Sam wants to play ball. Which plan is a compromise?","choices":["Play tag first, then ball","Only tag, and Sam must agree","Nobody plays at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a compromise, each friend gets some of what they want.","explain":"Tag first, then ball gives each friend a part. If only Mia gets her way, Sam just gives in."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} friends take turns on the slide. Each friend has 1 turn, then the next friend goes. How many turns is that?","answer":"n","hint":"Count 1 turn for each of the {n} friends.","explain":"Each of the {n} friends has 1 turn, so that is {=n} turns. Taking turns is one way a team cooperates.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A team is a group that works on one job. When we work together, we cooperate. Two friends can lift a big log that one friend cannot lift.","Some days friends want different things. Fox wants red. Owl wants blue. A compromise is a plan where each friend gets a part. They paint it half red and half blue. If only Fox gets his way, that is not a compromise.","To listen, look at the one who is talking. Wait until they stop. Then say back what you heard: \"You want a bridge?\" Now they know you heard them.","It is OK not to agree. Say it in a kind way: \"I see it a different way.\" Do not say \"Your idea is silly.\" And look out for friends on their own. Ask them to join in. That is how we include them."],"adult":["This lesson gives children the words for the habits that friendships and group work depend on. In England, the statutory Relationships Education guidance for primary schools asks pupils to learn about caring friendships and respectful relationships, including how to manage conflict and how to seek help. In the early years, the EYFS goal Building Relationships asks children to work and play cooperatively and take turns with others. The CASEL framework calls these relationship skills: listening well, cooperating, and resolving conflict constructively.","Key words for ages 5 to 8: team, cooperate, share, take turns, compromise, listen, active listening, agree, disagree respectfully, and include. Active listening means facing the speaker, not interrupting, and responding to what was actually said. A compromise means both sides give a little so that each gets part of what they wanted.","Common mistakes: thinking compromise means giving in, so the quieter child always loses out. Mixing up cooperate (working together on one job) with include (inviting someone in). Thinking that being quiet is the same as listening, even while playing with a toy. And thinking that disagreeing is always unkind, so children either stay silent or say \"Your idea is silly.\"","In the lab your child drags Fox and Bear to lift a log neither can lift alone. They choose fair plans when Fox and Owl want different things, and tap the signs that show Bear is listening. They sort kind and unkind ways to disagree, and drag Duck and Mouse into the team so the tower grows. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Next time two of you want different things, such as which film to watch, say \"Let's find a compromise\" and plan it together, like half of each or one now and one next time. Afterwards, ask your child: \"Did we both get some of what we wanted?\"","keyIdea":"Team words name the things good friends do together. Cooperate means working together on one job. Compromise means each friend gets some of what they want. Listen means look at the talker, wait, and say back what you heard. You can disagree kindly, with calm words. Include means asking others to join, so nobody is left out.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which team word fits? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🤝 Cooperate","⚖️ Compromise","🙋 Include"],"items":[["Two friends carry a heavy bag together",0],["We all tidy the room so it is done fast",0],["You pick the game today, I pick tomorrow",1],["Half red and half blue, so we both like it",1],["\"Come and play with us!\"",2],["Giving the new child a job in the game",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagteam","caption":"Flagship lab: lift a log together, find fair plans, spot real listening, sort kind ways to disagree, and include friends in the team.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_w4nSIDhIgC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a role in a group?","choices":["The job you do in the group","The name of the group","The prize the group wins"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the badges in the lab: crown, light bulb, helping hands and dove.","explain":"A role is your job in the group, like leader, ideas person, supporter or peacemaker."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Four friends all want to be the leader. What will most likely happen?","choices":["They argue, and little gets done","They finish very fast","Everyone is happy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the treehouse when every friend said “My way!”","explain":"Four leaders give four different orders, so nobody builds. One leader is enough, and the others do other jobs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Jo holds the box steady and says, “Well done!” Which role is Jo doing?","choices":["Supporter","Leader","Ideas person"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who helps build and cheers the others on?","explain":"Helping and cheering is the supporter’s job. The supporter makes the plan real."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Kai says, “First we get the paper, then we cut. Sam, you hold the glue.” Which role is that?","choices":["Leader","Peacemaker","Supporter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who makes the plan and gives out the jobs?","explain":"Making the plan and giving out jobs is what a leader does."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Is a supporter less important than the leader?","choices":["No, the group needs every role","Yes, only the leader matters","Yes, supporters just watch"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happened in the lab when the supporter sat out?","explain":"No. Without a supporter, nobody holds the planks and nothing gets built. Every role counts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Your group picks Mia’s idea, not yours. What helps the group most?","choices":["Help build Mia’s idea","Stop helping until they pick yours","Keep saying your idea is better"],"answer":0,"hint":"You do not always have to be in charge, or have your idea win.","explain":"Helping with the chosen idea is a real job: you are being a supporter. Pushing your idea again stops the work."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} friends all want to lead. Only 1 can be the leader. How many friends need to pick a different role?","answer":"n-1","hint":"One friend leads. Count the rest.","explain":"{n} take away the 1 leader is {=n-1}. Those {=n-1} friends can do ideas, help or keep the peace.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A team has a leader, a supporter and a peacemaker, but they cannot think what to make. Which role is missing?","choices":["Ideas person","Leader","Peacemaker"],"answer":0,"hint":"They are stuck for something new to try. Who thinks of new things?","explain":"The ideas person is missing. When a group is stuck, ask which job nobody is doing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"There are {n} groups in a class. Each group has 1 peacemaker. How many peacemakers are there?","answer":"n","hint":"Count 1 peacemaker for each of the {n} groups.","explain":"1 peacemaker in each of {n} groups makes {=n} peacemakers, so every group has someone to help when friends disagree.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Leo says, “What if we make the den into a pirate ship?” Which role is Leo doing?","choices":["Ideas person","Peacemaker","Supporter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is Leo making a plan, thinking of something new, helping, or calming an argument?","explain":"A pirate ship is a new thing to try, so Leo is the ideas person. A peacemaker helps friends who do not agree."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A team has {n} friends. Only 1 friend is the leader. How many friends do other jobs?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take the 1 leader away from the {n} friends.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}. Those {=n-1} friends can be the ideas person, supporters or the peacemaker.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ava and Ben both want to go first and start to shout. What does a peacemaker do?","choices":["Help each one say what they want, then find a fix","Say that Ava is right","Tell them both to go away"],"answer":0,"hint":"A peacemaker, or mediator, does not pick a winner.","explain":"The peacemaker helps both sides be heard, then helps them find a fix, like taking turns. Picking Ava as the winner is not mediating."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A team needs 4 roles: leader, ideas person, supporter and peacemaker. {n} of the roles have someone. How many roles still need someone?","answer":"4-n","hint":"Start with 4 roles and take away the {n} that are done.","explain":"4 take away {n} is {=4-n}. The team should find someone for each empty role so no job is missed.","params":{"n":[0,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A group is a team that works on one job. In a good group, each friend has a role. A role is the job you do in the group.","The leader keeps the plan. She says, “Floor first, then walls.” The ideas person says, “What if we add a ladder?” The supporter holds the plank and says, “Great job!” The peacemaker helps when friends do not agree. Another word for peacemaker is mediator.","What if all four friends want to lead? They all say, “My way!” Nobody builds, so nothing gets done. One leader is enough. The others pick a different job.","Every role counts. With no supporter, nobody holds the planks. With no peacemaker, a fight stops the work. You do not always have to be in charge. Some days you lead, and some days you help a friend’s idea."],"adult":["This lesson teaches the main roles children take in group work and why a group needs a mix of them. It sits in the Interactions strand of the IB PYP personal, social and physical education scope, and in the CASEL relationship skills of teamwork, collaborative problem-solving and showing leadership in groups. The PSHE Association programme of study for key stage 2 also asks pupils to work collaboratively and to resolve disagreements.","Key terms for ages 7 to 9: group, role, leader, ideas person (or idea-generator), supporter (sometimes called an encourager), peacemaker or mediator, take turns, compromise, and cooperate. Many classrooms use set cooperative-learning roles, such as facilitator, recorder, timekeeper and encourager. The names differ, but the idea is the same: each member has a clear job.","Common mistakes: thinking the leader is the most important person, so every child competes for it and the supporting jobs feel like losing. Thinking a mediator decides who is right, when a mediator helps both sides say what they want and then look for a fix both can accept. And thinking a role is fixed for life, rather than something a child can change from one task to the next.","In the lab your child hands out badges to four friends who all want to lead, until one leader is left and the treehouse is built. They match what friends say to each role, fix three stuck teams by adding the missing role, and take one role away at a time to see what breaks. Then they pick their own favourite role and read its strength and its watch-out. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Next time you cook or tidy up together, give out roles first: one leader who keeps the plan, one ideas person, one helper and one peacemaker. Swap roles next time, then ask your child which role felt best and why.","keyIdea":"People in a group can do different jobs, called roles. The leader keeps the plan and gives out jobs. The ideas person thinks of new things to try. The supporter helps build and cheers others on. The peacemaker, or mediator, helps both sides be heard and find a fix. A group works best with a mix of roles, not with everyone trying to lead.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which role is this friend doing? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👑 Leader","💡 Ideas person","🤝 Supporter","🕊️ Peacemaker"],"items":[["“First the floor, then the walls.”",0],["Gives each friend a job",0],["“What if we add a slide?”",1],["Thinks of a new way to fix it",1],["Holds the plank steady",2],["“Great job! Keep going!”",2],["“You talk first, then you.”",3],["Helps two friends find a fix",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagroles","caption":"Flagship lab: hand out badges to a team of leaders, match roles, add the missing one, and see what breaks without each.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HBcvu0UxYe":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which one is being a good friend?","choices":["Ella, who lets new children join","Max, who says: do what I say!","Jo, who only plays if you give her sweets"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for kind, fair and letting others join.","explain":"Ella lets others join, which is kind. Max is bossy, and a friend should not make you pay to play like Jo."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You and your friend had a big fall-out. Can you still be friends?","choices":["Yes, you can make up","No, never again","Only if they say sorry first"],"answer":0,"hint":"All friends fall out sometimes. What can you do to fix it?","explain":"Friends have ups and downs. Calm down, say sorry and play again. You do not have to wait for them to go first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You said you would play with Sam at break. Then Ben asks you to play. What does a good friend do?","choices":["Keep the promise and play with Sam","Go with Ben and say nothing","Hide from both of them"],"answer":0,"hint":"You made a promise. What does a friend you can count on do?","explain":"Keeping your promise means Sam can trust you. You could ask Ben to join you both."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A friend has {n} stickers and shares {k} with you. How many does your friend have left?","answer":"n-k","where":"k<n","hint":"Start with {n} stickers and take away the {k} your friend gave you.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}, so your friend has {=n-k} stickers left after sharing.","params":{"n":[5,10],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A boy whispers and points at a girl, then laughs. Is he being a good friend?","choices":["No, that is mean","Yes, it is only a joke","Yes, if his friends laugh too"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how the girl feels when someone points and laughs at her.","explain":"Whispering, pointing and laughing at someone is mean. It makes her feel sad and left out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Kim calls you a mean name, then says sorry. What could you do?","choices":["Say thank you for saying sorry, and play again","Call her a mean name back","Tell everyone not to play with her"],"answer":0,"hint":"She said sorry. How do friends make up?","explain":"Saying sorry is how friends fix a fall-out. Accepting it lets you play again. Being mean back keeps the fall-out going."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which word means a friend who keeps their promises?","choices":["Someone you can count on","Bossy","Sneaky"],"answer":0,"hint":"If they say they will do it, do they do it?","explain":"A friend you can count on does what they said. Bossy means always telling others what to do."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"{a} friends are playing tag. {b} of them have to go home. How many are still playing?","answer":"a-b","where":"b<a","hint":"Start with {a} friends and take away the {b} who went home.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}, so {=a-b} friends are still playing tag.","params":{"a":[4,9],"b":[1,3]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You want to play blocks. Your friend wants to play tag. What does a good friend do?","choices":["Take turns: tag first, then blocks","Shout until they play blocks","Go off and play alone in a huff"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good friend is fair. How can you both get a go?","explain":"Taking turns to choose is fair, so you both get to play your game. Shouting to get your way is bossy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{a} children are playing a game. {b} more children ask to join, and the group says yes. How many children are playing now?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Good friends let others join. Add the new children to the group.","explain":"{a} children and {b} more who joined makes {=a+b} children playing together.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You broke your friend's crayon by mistake. What does a good friend do?","choices":["Tell them and say sorry","Hide it and say nothing","Say someone else did it"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good friend is honest, even after a mistake.","explain":"Telling the truth and saying sorry is honest. Your friend can trust you, and you can sort it out together."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You and your friend take turns to choose the game. There are {n} turns at playtime. How many turns do you get to choose?","answer":"n/2","where":"n%2==0","hint":"Turns go me, you, me, you. Split the turns into two fair groups.","explain":"{n} turns shared fairly between two friends is {=n/2} turns each.","params":{"n":[2,10]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A good friend is kind. They say nice things and help you. A good friend lets you join in the game.","A good friend is fair. If you want different games, you take turns to choose. Bossy means you always get your way. That is not fair.","A good friend tells the truth, even after a mistake. They keep their promises. If they say they will save you a seat, they do.","Friends fall out sometimes. That is OK. Take a big breath. Say sorry. Then play again. You can still be friends."],"adult":["This topic is the 'Caring friendships' strand of the DfE guidance for Relationships Education (2019). Pupils learn what good friendships are like: respect, truth, trust, loyalty, kindness and sharing. They also learn that most friendships have ups and downs, and that these can often be worked through. The PSHE Association Key Stage 1 programme and the IB PYP strand on interactions set the same goals for ages 5 to 7.","At this age a friend is often whoever a child is playing with right now. So the lesson names the qualities that make a friendship last. These are kindness, honesty, keeping promises (being reliable), fairness (taking turns to choose) and letting others join (inclusion). It also names unfriendly acts a child can spot: being bossy, unkind words and leaving people out.","Common mistakes: thinking one falling out ends a friendship for good; treating friendship as a deal, as in 'you can play if you give me your car'; thinking a good friend always agrees with you; and fibbing to dodge trouble after an accident. Be careful not to call a child a bad friend. Talk about what they did, and show how to repair it: calm down, say sorry, then play again.","In the lab your child pins good-friend badges on a friend. They choose fair and kind moves when games clash or someone asks to join. They own up and keep a promise, put the steps of making up in order, and spot the unfriendly picture in a strip."]},"familyTip":"Over tea, ask: \"Who was a good friend to you today? What did they do?\" Then ask: \"When were you a good friend?\" Name the quality, such as kind, honest or fair.","keyIdea":"A good friend is kind, honest, fair and someone you can count on: they keep promises and let others join in. Being bossy, saying mean things and leaving people out are not friendly. All friends fall out sometimes. You can make up: calm down, say sorry, then play again.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Good friend or not friendly? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Good friend","Not friendly"],"items":[["Takes turns choosing",0],["Always bossy",1],["Keeps a promise",0],["Says you can't play",1],["Tells the truth",0],["Says mean words",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfriend","caption":"Flagship lab: pin good-friend badges, choose fair and honest moves, put the steps of making up in order, and spot unfriendly behaviour.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_u1lNk1HXNo":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You took Kai's crayon. What fixes it?","choices":["Say sorry and give it back","Say sorry and keep it","Hide it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sorry is the start. What does Kai need back?","explain":"Saying sorry is good, but Kai needs his crayon. Giving it back puts it right."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You spilled Raj's juice. What helps most?","choices":["Say sorry and help wipe it up","Say: The cup did it!","Walk away"],"answer":0,"hint":"What would make Raj feel better about the mess?","explain":"Saying sorry and wiping up the juice helps fix the oops. Blaming the cup is a fib."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia said sorry and helped fix your tower. What can you say?","choices":["That is OK. Let us play!","Go away for ever!","Say sorry ten more times!"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mia said sorry and she helped. How do you make up?","explain":"One real sorry is enough. Saying 'That is OK' lets you both play again."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You said Jo's picture is ugly. Jo is sad. What helps?","choices":["Say sorry and say something kind about it","Say it again louder","Say nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Words can hurt too. What words could help instead?","explain":"Mean words hurt. Say sorry, then say something kind, like 'I like the big sun.'"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{a} children got hurt in the game. You have said sorry to {b} of them. How many more need a sorry?","answer":"a-b","where":"b<a","hint":"Everyone who got hurt needs a sorry. Take away the ones you have done.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}, so {=a-b} more children need a sorry from you.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which child needs a sorry?","choices":["Ana, who you bumped by mistake","Kai, who is happy on the swing","Ella, who is reading a book"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the child who got hurt.","explain":"Ana got hurt, even though it was a mistake, so she needs a sorry. Kai and Ella are fine."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia's tower had {n} blocks. It fell. You and Mia build {k} blocks back. How many blocks tall is the tower now?","answer":"k","where":"k<=n","hint":"Count only the blocks that are back on the tower.","explain":"You built {k} blocks back, so the tower is {k} blocks tall now. Mia has {=n-k} more to go.","params":{"n":[3,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Ben shouts 'sorry!' and runs off. Mia's tower is still on the floor. Is that enough?","choices":["No. Stay and help build it again.","Yes. He said the word.","Yes, if he says it fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the tower fixed? Is Mia still sad?","explain":"A sorry is the start. Ben should stay and help Mia build the tower again."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You knocked down Mia's tower. Which is the best sorry?","choices":["Sorry, Mia. I knocked your tower down.","It fell by itself!","Ha ha, it fell!"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good sorry says what you did. Which one does that?","explain":"Saying what you did shows Mia that you know what went wrong, and that you mean your sorry."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You knocked over a tower of {n} blocks. You help build it again. You put back {k} blocks. How many blocks are still on the floor?","answer":"n-k","where":"k<n","hint":"Start with {n} blocks on the floor. Take away the {k} you put back.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}, so {=n-k} blocks are still on the floor. Keep helping until the tower is back up.","params":{"n":[3,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You bumped Zoe by mistake. Her knee hurts. What do you do?","choices":["Say sorry and get a grown-up","Nothing. It was a mistake.","Say: You are fine, get up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Zoe is still hurt, even if you did not mean it.","explain":"A mistake can still hurt. Say sorry, then help: get a grown-up or a plaster for her knee."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You took {n} of Leo's cars. You give {k} back. How many more cars do you need to give back?","answer":"n-k","where":"k<n","hint":"Leo needs all {n} cars back. You gave {k}. How many are left to give?","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}. Give back {=n-k} more cars so Leo has all of his cars again.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Oops! Ben's ball hits Mia's tower. It falls down. Mia cries. Ben did not mean to do it. But Mia is still sad.","Ben says, \"Sorry, Mia. I knocked your tower down.\" He says what he did. That shows he means it. Mia is still a bit sad. So Ben helps. They build the tower again, block by block. Now Mia smiles.","Each oops has its own fix. Took a toy? Give it back. Bumped a friend? Get a grown-up or a plaster. Said a mean word? Say a kind one.","When a friend says sorry to you, you can say, \"That is OK.\" Then you can play again."],"adult":["This topic sits in Personal, Social and Emotional Development in the EYFS (Development Matters 2023 and the Early Learning Goals 'Building Relationships' and 'Managing Self'). It also links to the 'Respectful relationships' and 'Caring friendships' strands of DfE Relationships Education (2019) at Key Stage 1. At 4 to 5 years, children say sorry when a grown-up prompts them. The goal is that they also try to make amends: do something that repairs the harm.","A full apology for this age has three parts. First, name what you did: 'I knocked your tower down.' Second, say sorry. Third, offer a repair that fits the harm. Rebuild what was broken, return what was taken, fetch help for a hurt, or swap a hurtful word for a kind one. Many settings use this restorative approach in place of a bare 'say sorry'. The child who was hurt can then accept the apology, which is the start of forgiveness and playing together again.","Common mistakes: thinking an accident needs no sorry (a hurt is still a hurt, whatever the intent); a quick 'sorry' shouted while running off; saying sorry but keeping the toy; and thinking a sorry must be repeated over and over before you can make up. Be careful not to force a child to say a sorry they cannot yet feel. Calm first, then help them notice the other child's face, then model the words.","In the lab your child picks the sorry that names what was done. They drag the right fix-it to a sad friend: blocks, the teddy or a plaster. Then they find every child in a playground who got hurt, mend hurt words and a spill, and choose how to accept a sorry and play again."]},"familyTip":"Next time there is a toy-room oops, try the three steps together: \"What happened? Say sorry and say what you did. How can we fix it?\" Praise the fix, not just the word sorry.","keyIdea":"When you hurt or upset someone, even by accident, say sorry and say what you did: \"Sorry, Mia. I knocked your tower down.\" Then do something to put it right: build the tower again, give the toy back, or get help for a bump. When someone says sorry to you, you can say \"That is OK\" and play again.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it help make up? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Helps make up","Does not help"],"items":[["Sorry, I took your car",0],["Give the car back",0],["Run away",1],["Build the tower again",0],["Say: It was not me!",1],["Laugh at the mess",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsorry","caption":"Flagship lab: pick a good sorry, drag the right fix-it to a sad friend, say sorry to everyone who got hurt, and make up.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_iWGnyUyN2j":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Leo says: I want tag because we played football yesterday. Which reply shows you listened?","choices":["So you want tag because we had football yesterday?","But I want football!","That is a silly reason."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which reply says back Leo's reason?","explain":"Saying back his reason shows Leo you heard him. Then he is more ready to hear you too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Mo and Jen both want to be the goalie. Which fix can they both accept?","choices":["Swap at half time","Mo is goalie because he asked first","Nobody is goalie"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the fix where both get some of what they want.","explain":"Swapping at half time gives both a turn. Mo always winning leaves Jen with nothing, and nobody being goalie is bad for both."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam always lets Ali choose, so they never argue. Is that a fair fix?","choices":["No: Sam should get a say too","Yes: not arguing is all that matters","Yes: Ali is louder"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does Sam get anything he wants?","explain":"Always giving in keeps the peace but is not fair to Sam. A good fix gives both friends a say."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which one needs a grown-up straight away?","choices":["Someone is hitting","Both want the blue pen","You want different games"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is anyone getting hurt, or could they be?","explain":"Hitting is never OK and someone could get hurt, so get a grown-up. Pens and games you can usually sort out together."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Ava and Leo take turns on the swing: {k} pushes each. Ava has had her {k} pushes. How many pushes does Leo get now?","answer":"k","hint":"Taking turns fairly means the same number each.","explain":"Fair turns mean the same each, so Leo gets {k} pushes too. Then it is Ava's turn again.","params":{"k":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ava says: You always ruin everything! What would be a better way to say it?","choices":["I feel cross because I wanted a turn to choose.","You ruin everything, and I mean it.","Say nothing and sulk."],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with I feel, then give the reason.","explain":"An I-statement tells Leo how you feel and why, without blaming him, so he does not need to fight back."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"While Leo explains his side, what should Ava do?","choices":["Listen to the end, then say back what he said","Plan what she will say next","Talk over him so she is heard"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can Ava show she really heard him?","explain":"Listening to the end and saying back his reason shows Leo she heard him. Planning your reply means you miss what he says."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Ava and Leo try {t} fixes. {w} of them give one friend everything and the other nothing. The rest give both enough. How many fixes can they both accept?","answer":"t-w","where":"w<t","hint":"A fix that gives one friend nothing will not be accepted. Take those away.","explain":"{t} take away {w} is {=t-w}. Only the {=t-w} fixes that give both friends enough are ones they can both accept.","params":{"t":[3,6],"w":[1,2]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ava and Leo are shouting about which game to play. What should they do first?","choices":["Calm down, with slow breaths or a short break","Keep shouting until one gives in","Ask everyone to pick a side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you think of a good fix while you are very cross?","explain":"When you are very cross it is hard to think. Calming down first makes it possible to talk and listen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ava takes {b} slow breaths. Each breath brings her cross-o-meter down 2 points. How many points does it go down?","answer":"2*b","hint":"Each breath is 2 points. Add 2 for every breath, or multiply.","explain":"{b} breaths times 2 points is {=2*b} points. Slow breaths really do help your body calm down.","params":{"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is an I-statement that does not blame?","choices":["I feel left out because I did not get the ball.","You never pass to me!","You are the worst player ever."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one that starts with how I feel and gives a reason.","explain":"I feel left out because ... says your feeling and the reason. It does not blame, so your friend can listen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Break is {n} minutes. Ava and Leo agree to split it fairly: tag for half, then football for half. How many minutes of tag do they play?","answer":"n/2","where":"n%2==0","hint":"Half of the break is tag. Halve {n}.","explain":"Half of {n} is {=n/2}, so they play tag for {=n/2} minutes, then football for {=n/2}. Both get enough.","params":{"n":[10,30]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Ava wants football. Leo wants tag. They both get cross and start to shout. Shouting will not fix it. First they need to calm down. Take some slow breaths, count to five, or step back for a minute.","Next, Ava says how she feels and why. She starts with I: \"I feel cross because we never play my game.\" That is called an I-statement. It does not blame Leo, so he does not need to fight back.","Then Ava listens to Leo all the way to the end. He says, \"We played football yesterday.\" Ava says it back: \"So you want tag because we had football yesterday?\" Now Leo knows she heard him.","Last, they look for a fix that works for both. Football all break is good for Ava but bad for Leo. Nobody playing is bad for both. Tag first, then football, gives them both enough. That is a compromise. If someone hits, gets hurt, or you are stuck, get a grown-up."],"adult":["This topic sits in DfE Relationships Education (2019), 'Caring friendships': most friendships have ups and downs that can be worked through, violence is never right, and pupils learn to manage conflict and seek help when needed. The PSHE Association Programme of Study covers it at Key Stage 2 under Relationships (friendships and safe relationships). In CASEL's framework it is the relationship skill 'resolving conflicts constructively'.","The lab follows a common school conflict-resolution routine. First calm down, because a child flooded with anger cannot problem-solve. Then use an I-statement (feeling plus reason, no blame), and active or reflective listening (letting the other child finish, then saying back their reason). Last, negotiate a compromise in which each side gives a little so both get enough. That is a 'win-win' rather than 'win-lose' or 'lose-lose'.","Common mistakes: trying to sort it out while still shouting; 'you' statements that blame ('You always ruin it'); planning a reply instead of listening; thinking the fair fix is whoever asked first or shouts loudest; and the quiet child who always gives in to keep the peace. That is not a fair fix, and it is worth gently noticing. Be careful, too, that children know some things are not theirs to sort out: hitting, someone being hurt or scared, or being stuck after trying. Those need a trusted adult.","In the lab your child brings Ava's cross-o-meter down with calm-down moves, picks the I-statement, picks the reply that says back Leo's reason, drags fixes onto a table and watches both friends' star meters, and sorts fall-outs friends can fix from ones that need a grown-up."]},"familyTip":"Next time siblings or friends fall out, try the four steps out loud: \"Calm first. Now each of you say I feel ... because ... . Say back what you heard. What fix gives you both enough?\" Praise the fix they find, not just the quiet.","keyIdea":"Friends fall out sometimes, and you can sort most of it out yourselves in four steps. Calm down first. Say how you feel and why, without blaming: \"I feel cross because I wanted a turn.\" Listen to your friend's side to the end, then say back what you heard. Then find a fix that gives you both enough, such as taking turns. If someone is hitting or hurt, or you are stuck, get a grown-up.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it help sort out a fall-out? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Helps sort it out","Makes it worse"],"items":[["Take slow breaths first",0],["Shout until they give in",1],["I feel sad because I was left out",0],["You always ruin it!",1],["Take turns",0],["Walk off for ever",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagagree","caption":"Flagship lab: calm the cross-o-meter, say an I-statement, listen and say back, find a fix that fills both star meters, and know when to get a grown-up.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
